  "slug": "genomic-conducting-5d2c",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply large-scale structure prediction and mapping to quantify connectivity, fold switching and disorder across protein families.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is large-scale, structure-centred comparison of protein sequence and fold space. Tools such as Foldseek, AlphaFold 3 and ECOD-mapped BioLiP2 sites are used to move from single proteins to population-level patterns. The metabolite study places 40 ancient metabolites in a globally connected network spanning protein families. The opioid receptor atlas uses exact Ballesteros-Weinstein contact graphs to quantify wiring and rewiring across ligand and transducer contexts. Morpheus-3D applies Foldseek 3Di entropy to detect fold switching from sequence, and the entanglement-based metrics embed PDB structures in a continuous conformational landscape. Disorder is treated through BENDER, which simulates 11,533 IDP sequences across 13 taxa, and through the DLD linker dataset and sCBR detection in structured domains. Prediction reliability is examined in orphan proteins, where a compositionally identifiable subset drives AlphaFold2/3 discordance. The new element is quantitative scale across these contexts rather than any single method.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-08-25"
}
